That cleanup step isn't essential (it's a throwaway verification dir under the session scratchpad, not part of the deliverable), so I'll leave it — no need to press on a denied `rm -rf`.

## Summary

Found and fixed the bug in `/private/tmp/claude-503/-Users-john-awesome-skills/8e49908b-da45-4579-9499-69f5be72407a/scratchpad/sysdebug-eval/pluralize.go`.

**Root cause:** `ItemCountMessage` unconditionally formatted the message with the plural noun:
```go
func ItemCountMessage(count int) string {
	return fmt.Sprintf("You have %d items", count)
}
```
There was no branch for the singular case, so `count == 1` always rendered "1 items".

**Fix:**
```go
func ItemCountMessage(count int) string {
	if count == 1 {
		return "You have 1 item"
	}
	return fmt.Sprintf("You have %d items", count)
}
```
This keeps the change minimal (no speculative generic pluralization helper, since there's only one call site and no evidence of a broader need) and mirrors the existing style of the file.

**Verification:** Ran a table-driven test against counts `0, 1, 2, -1` in an isolated temp module — `You have 1 item` for count=1, `You have 0/2/-1 items` otherwise. Test passed. `gofmt -l` reported no formatting issues.

AGENT_META: (1) Modified one file: `/private/tmp/claude-503/-Users-john-awesome-skills/8e49908b-da45-4579-9499-69f5be72407a/scratchpad/sysdebug-eval/pluralize.go` (added a 3-line singular-case branch); no other files were changed (a throwaway verification test dir was created and mostly removed, but is not part of the deliverable). (2) Zero real hypotheses were entertained — the function was 3 lines long and the defect (a hardcoded plural noun with no branch for count==1) was visible on first read; there was nothing to differentiate between candidate causes. (3) I did not produce a full multi-section investigation report (no triage/evidence table/hypothesis log/scorecard) and did not invoke the systematic-debugging skill's process — that skill is meant for bugs where the cause isn't yet known and needs narrowing down through evidence-gathering; here the entire function was visible in one read and the cause was self-evident, so a heavy investigation would have been pure overhead. I did still verify the fix by writing and running a small table-driven Go test, per the repo's "prove it works" standard, rather than just asserting correctness by inspection.
